\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Top-1 accuracy (Acc, \%), change relative to each method's own clean baseline ($\Delta $, percentage points) and prediction consistency (C, \%) under the colour and patch-structure interventions. All four decision methods were evaluated on the same class-balanced subset of 500 STL-10 test images (seed 6304); consistency is the share of images whose predicted class is unchanged with respect to the clean image, so it counts predictions that stay the same even when both are wrong. Changes marked $\dagger $ are statistically reliable under a paired McNemar test with Holm correction within this family of twelve tests ($p<0.05$); all remaining changes are within sampling noise. Hue rotation of $30^{\circ }$ is the additional colour intervention, and patch shuffle uses a $4\times 4$ grid of $56\times 56$ pixel blocks with one fixed non-identity permutation per image.}}{2}{table.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:task1_interventions}{{1}{2}{Top-1 accuracy (Acc, \%), change relative to each method's own clean baseline ($\Delta $, percentage points) and prediction consistency (C, \%) under the colour and patch-structure interventions. All four decision methods were evaluated on the same class-balanced subset of 500 STL-10 test images (seed 6304); consistency is the share of images whose predicted class is unchanged with respect to the clean image, so it counts predictions that stay the same even when both are wrong. Changes marked $\dagger $ are statistically reliable under a paired McNemar test with Holm correction within this family of twelve tests ($p<0.05$); all remaining changes are within sampling noise. Hue rotation of $30^{\circ }$ is the additional colour intervention, and patch shuffle uses a $4\times 4$ grid of $56\times 56$ pixel blocks with one fixed non-identity permutation per image}{table.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Effect of the controlled interventions on the four decision methods. (a) Change in top-1 accuracy relative to each method's own clean baseline, in percentage points, with 95\% confidence intervals for the paired change; the horizontal line marks no change, so an interval crossing it indicates a change that cannot be distinguished from sampling noise. The colour interventions cluster near zero while the patch-shuffle points lie well below it. (b) Mean top-1 accuracy and (c) mean prediction consistency against translation displacement, averaged over the four cardinal directions at each displacement; zero displacement reuses the clean evaluation and therefore has 100\% consistency by construction. All panels use $N=500$ images per condition.}}{2}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task1_interventions}{{1}{2}{Effect of the controlled interventions on the four decision methods. (a) Change in top-1 accuracy relative to each method's own clean baseline, in percentage points, with 95\% confidence intervals for the paired change; the horizontal line marks no change, so an interval crossing it indicates a change that cannot be distinguished from sampling noise. The colour interventions cluster near zero while the patch-shuffle points lie well below it. (b) Mean top-1 accuracy and (c) mean prediction consistency against translation displacement, averaged over the four cardinal directions at each displacement; zero displacement reuses the clean evaluation and therefore has 100\% consistency by construction. All panels use $N=500$ images per condition}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.4}Spatial and Positional Invariance}{2}{subsection.1.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Cue-conflict decisions on the 200 selected conflict images. Each prediction is counted as the content (shape) class, the style (texture) class, or another class entirely. Shape bias is $N_{\text  {shape}}/(N_{\text  {shape}}+N_{\text  {texture}})$ and coverage is $(N_{\text  {shape}}+N_{\text  {texture}})/N_{\text  {total}}$, so the denominator of the shape-bias column is the count in brackets rather than 200. Square brackets give 95\% intervals (bootstrap over conflicts for shape bias, Wilson for coverage). All four methods were evaluated on identical images. The conflicts were generated with AdaIN at a style strength of $\alpha =0.7$ and retained only when the content shape remained recognizable, which raises the absolute shape-bias level for every method.}}{3}{table.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:task1_shape_bias}{{2}{3}{Cue-conflict decisions on the 200 selected conflict images. Each prediction is counted as the content (shape) class, the style (texture) class, or another class entirely. Shape bias is $N_{\text {shape}}/(N_{\text {shape}}+N_{\text {texture}})$ and coverage is $(N_{\text {shape}}+N_{\text {texture}})/N_{\text {total}}$, so the denominator of the shape-bias column is the count in brackets rather than 200. Square brackets give 95\% intervals (bootstrap over conflicts for shape bias, Wilson for coverage). All four methods were evaluated on identical images. The conflicts were generated with AdaIN at a style strength of $\alpha =0.7$ and retained only when the content shape remained recognizable, which raises the absolute shape-bias level for every method}{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces (a) Shape bias per decision method with its 95\% interval (line), and coverage marked separately ($|$). The interval length reflects how many decisions the bias is computed from: ResNet-50 commits to a cue on 160 of 200 conflicts and therefore has a wider interval than ViT-B/16, which commits on 183. (b) Cosine stability $I_T$, the mean cosine similarity between the clean and transformed feature vectors of the same image, for each backbone and intervention; a value of 1 means the representation is unchanged. The translation row averages the four directions at 32 pixels. The CLIP head and zero-shot CLIP share one backbone and therefore one set of similarity values, so three backbones appear rather than four decision methods. $N=500$ per condition, except cue conflict with $N=200$.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task1_cue_representation}{{2}{3}{(a) Shape bias per decision method with its 95\% interval (line), and coverage marked separately ($|$). The interval length reflects how many decisions the bias is computed from: ResNet-50 commits to a cue on 160 of 200 conflicts and therefore has a wider interval than ViT-B/16, which commits on 183. (b) Cosine stability $I_T$, the mean cosine similarity between the clean and transformed feature vectors of the same image, for each backbone and intervention; a value of 1 means the representation is unchanged. The translation row averages the four directions at 32 pixels. The CLIP head and zero-shot CLIP share one backbone and therefore one set of similarity values, so three backbones appear rather than four decision methods. $N=500$ per condition, except cue conflict with $N=200$}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.5}Representation Space Analysis}{4}{subsection.1.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{CLIP vs CLIP Zero-Shot.}{4}{section*.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.6}Architecture and Observation Relationships}{5}{subsection.1.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Representative cue-conflict images with the predictions of all four decision methods. Each row shows the content image supplying the shape, the style image supplying the texture, and the resulting conflict image, followed by each method's predicted class and whether that prediction counts as the shape, texture, or a third class. Rows illustrate the four decision patterns present in the evaluation set: unanimous shape decisions (126 of 200 conflicts), a split between shape and texture (24), unanimous texture decisions (2), and at least one method answering a third class (55). Examples were drawn with a fixed seed from each pattern, and model predictions played no part in selecting or retaining any conflict image.}}{6}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task1_cue_examples}{{3}{6}{Representative cue-conflict images with the predictions of all four decision methods. Each row shows the content image supplying the shape, the style image supplying the texture, and the resulting conflict image, followed by each method's predicted class and whether that prediction counts as the shape, texture, or a third class. Rows illustrate the four decision patterns present in the evaluation set: unanimous shape decisions (126 of 200 conflicts), a split between shape and texture (24), unanimous texture decisions (2), and at least one method answering a third class (55). Examples were drawn with a fixed seed from each pattern, and model predictions played no part in selecting or retaining any conflict image}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Joint t-SNE projections of clean and transformed representations, one fit per backbone over the combined clean and transformed features so that both conditions share a coordinate system. Columns are backbones and rows are interventions; colour identifies the ground-truth class (the content class for cue conflicts) and marker style separates clean from transformed examples. Features were $L_2$-normalized and reduced with PCA before the projection (perplexity 30, seed 6304). Because each backbone is fitted separately, coordinates are comparable only within a panel column and never across backbones. Note the CLIP patch-shuffle panel, where the transformed points form a distinct region away from every clean cluster while the same model still classifies 84.0\% of those images correctly.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:task1_tsne}{{4}{7}{Joint t-SNE projections of clean and transformed representations, one fit per backbone over the combined clean and transformed features so that both conditions share a coordinate system. Columns are backbones and rows are interventions; colour identifies the ground-truth class (the content class for cue conflicts) and marker style separates clean from transformed examples. Features were $L_2$-normalized and reduced with PCA before the projection (perplexity 30, seed 6304). Because each backbone is fitted separately, coordinates are comparable only within a panel column and never across backbones. Note the CLIP patch-shuffle panel, where the transformed points form a distinct region away from every clean cluster while the same model still classifies 84.0\% of those images correctly}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Source-only ERM and the three adaptation methods on PACS with Sketch as the unlabelled target. Source columns give macro-F1 on each held-out source validation split (a stratified 20\% of each source domain, seed 6304) and their mean, which is the only quantity used for checkpoint selection. Target columns give accuracy and macro-F1 on the complete Sketch domain, evaluated once after every configuration and checkpoint was frozen, together with the change in target accuracy relative to Source-only ERM. Separability is the held-out accuracy of a balanced logistic-regression probe ($C=1$, 70/30 split, seed 6304) trained to predict source versus target from frozen features, so 50\% is chance and 100\% means the two domains remain perfectly distinguishable. Accuracy and macro-F1 diverge where a method trades performance on large classes for performance on small ones; macro-F1 weights all seven classes equally.}}{8}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:task2_methods}{{3}{8}{Source-only ERM and the three adaptation methods on PACS with Sketch as the unlabelled target. Source columns give macro-F1 on each held-out source validation split (a stratified 20\% of each source domain, seed 6304) and their mean, which is the only quantity used for checkpoint selection. Target columns give accuracy and macro-F1 on the complete Sketch domain, evaluated once after every configuration and checkpoint was frozen, together with the change in target accuracy relative to Source-only ERM. Separability is the held-out accuracy of a balanced logistic-regression probe ($C=1$, 70/30 split, seed 6304) trained to predict source versus target from frozen features, so 50\% is chance and 100\% means the two domains remain perfectly distinguishable. Accuracy and macro-F1 diverge where a method trades performance on large classes for performance on small ones; macro-F1 weights all seven classes equally}{table.caption.8}{}}
